xlink:href="http://www.biomart.org"/></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>BeanShell (Lightweight Scripting for Java)</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.beanshell.org/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Romano</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Bertolini</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>De Paoli</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Fattore</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Marra</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Mauri</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Merelli</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Porro</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Scaglione</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Milanesi</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Network integration of data and analysis of oncology interest</article-title><source>Journal of Integrative Bioinformatics</source><year>2006</year><volume>0021</volume><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://journal.imbio.de/index.php?paper_id=21"/></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Biowep portal</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://bioinformatics.istge.it:8080/biowep/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Olivier</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>The IARC TP53 Database: new online mutation analysis and recommendations to users</article-title><source>Hum Mutat</source><year>2002</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>607</fpage><lpage>14</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12007217</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/humu.10081</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>IARC TP53 Mutation Database</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www-p53.iarc.fr/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Romano</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Kracht</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Manniello</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Stegehuis</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Fritze</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The role of informatics in the coordinated management of biological resources collections</article-title><source>Applied Bioinformatics</source><year>2005</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>175</fpage><lpage>86</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16231959</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>CABRI catalogues of biological resources</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cabri.org/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Romano</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Marra</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Milanesi</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Web services and workflow management for biological resources</article-title><source>BMC Bioinformatics</source><year>2005</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>s24</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16351751</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1471-2105-6-S4-S24</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Biowep support site</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://bioinformatics.istge.it/biowep"/></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bartocci</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Corradini</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Merelli</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Scortichini</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>BioWMS: a web based Workflow Management System for Bioinformatics</article-title><source>BMC Bioinformatics</source><year>2007</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>S2</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17430564</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1471-2105-8-S1-S2</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Corradini</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Merelli</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Hermes: agent-base middleware for mobile computing</article-title><source>Mobile Computing, LNCS</source><year>2005</year><volume>3465</volume><fpage>234</fpage><lpage>270</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.springerlink.com/content/pbwkbq813t5qwm51/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Taverna Workbench</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://taverna.sourceforge.net/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>FreeFluo enactor engine</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://freefluo.sourceforge.net/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Java Run-time Environment, JRE</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://java.sun.com/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Graphviz visualization tool</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.graphviz.org/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Apache Tomcat</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://tomcat.apache.org/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B31"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>mySQL database management system</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.mysql.com/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B32"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>mySQL-connector</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.mysql.com/products/connector/j/"/></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>List of workflows available in the biowep workflow repository</bold>. In this figure, the web page listing all workflows is shown. Each item in the list includes the name of the workflow, a short description of the same, its type (this information also determines which engine must execute it) and its current version. Also available for each item are two buttons: one for launching the workflow and one for displaying its detailed description.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>Details on a workflow included in the repository</bold>. In this figure, the web page including the detailed description of a workflow is shown. This description includes information both at the workflow and at the version levels. The first level has data such as the author and the run contexts, while the second level includes, among other information, the annotation of the workflow. A link to the diagram of the workflow is also available.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>"Search by ontology" page, allowing to search workflows by their annotations</bold>. In this figure, the web page allowing for the "search by ontology" is shown. Columns include, from left to right, the application domain, the task, the input and the output. Multiple conditions that are set on each column are combined by OR, and conditions that are imposed on different columns are combined by AND. Results of the search are displayed in the lower part of the same page and include both workflows and single steps.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>I<bold>nput form for executing a workflow</bold>. In this figure, the input form for the execution of a workflow is shown. Input fields are described in details and suggestions for input values are given. Required and optional fields are pointed out.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>List of results of previous executions</bold>. In this figure, the web page listing all results is shown. For each execution, input values are reported. Results of all elaboration steps are listed and can be seen (by using a java library that must be downloaded and installed locally). A link to the diagram of the workflow is also available.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>H</bold><bold>om</bold><bold>e page of biowep support site</bold>. In this figure, the home page of the support web site is shown. Interested researchers can retrieve from the site all available documentation. They can also download software, database structure, workflows and submit their workflows. The same account can be used for the support site and the portal.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><bold>The conceptual architecture of biowep</bold>. The architecture of biowep includes three components. The Workflow Manager (WM) for the creation of predefined workflows by using Taverna or BioWMS, and their annotation that is done by using a classification of bioinformatics tasks (ontology). The User Interface (UI) supports end users authentication and profiling and selection and enactment of workflows. This can be carried out by searching workflows annotations. The Workflow Executor (WE) enacts workflows by using FreFluo or by submitting them to BioAgent/Hermes. In this figure, red blocks refer to open source software already available, light blue block refer to original software/database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p><bold>The biowep entity &#x02013; relationships &#x02013; attributes schema</bold>. In this schema, entities and relationships are coloured on the basis of their prevalent entity: yellow for data on users, magenta for information on workflows and cyan for the results of executions. It is important to note links between users' domains of interests and workflows' domains of application, as well as between users' roles and workflows' run contexts. Also, it should be noticed the difference between workflows and their versions and how the annotations and the executions data refer to versions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-8-S1-S19-8"/></fig></sec></back></article>